+
+# Keep post revisions on every edit
+
+## Context
+
+Saving the edit form overwrote a post for good, and authors lost work to
+accidental overwrites. Editorial asked for a history of earlier versions of
+each post and a one-click restore, private to the post's author.
+
+## Decision
+
+Every write that replaces a post's title, excerpt and body goes through
+`PostRevision.replaceContent()`, which first copies the version being replaced
+into `post_revisions`. The edit form and a restore are the two callers.
+
+- **The copy and the write share one transaction**, and the post is read
+  inside it. A failed write keeps no revision, and the version kept is the one
+  actually overwritten rather than the one the request loaded earlier.
+- **Revisions are append-only.** A restore copies a revision back onto the
+  post; it never moves or removes the revision, and it keeps the version it
+  replaces like any other edit.
+- **Every successful save keeps a revision, including one that changes
+  nothing.** Skipping unchanged saves would need a rule for restores of
+  identical content too; keeping all of them is the rule with no exceptions.
+- **History has its own policy ability**, `manageRevisions`, rather than
+  reusing `update`. A restore must pass both.
+- **A revision is always looked up together with its post id**, so a revision
+  of another post is a 404 whoever owns that post.
+- **Deleting a post deletes its revisions in the controller**, in one
+  transaction. The foreign key also declares `ON DELETE CASCADE`, but SQLite
+  only honours it on connections that enable foreign keys, which this app's
+  connection does not.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Nothing an author saves is lost while the post exists. The table grows with
+every save, unchanged ones included, and nothing prunes it — pruning was out
+of scope. Any new code path that writes a post's content has to call
+`replaceContent()` to stay in the history; a direct `Post.update()` bypasses
+it.
